{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering decriminalization experiment is set to end, and a new law will reclassify possessing small amounts of hard drugs as a crime. The new law, HB4002, offers individuals caught with illicit substances the option to undergo treatment or face possession charges.\n\n*** \nThose who opt for treatment must complete a behavioral health program and participate in a deflection program to avoid fines. The law makes personal-use possession a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. It also introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near public areas like parks.\n\n*** \nProponents argue that the law allows counties to develop tailored programs based on their resources. However, opponents claim it may create inconsistent policies across the state. The law encourages, but does not require, counties to establish treatment alternatives to divert individuals from the justice system.\n\n*** \nAs the law takes effect, individuals will be connected to a mobile behavioral health outreach team within 30 minutes. As people navigate this new law, they may need to be mindful of their surroundings, much like when they're out shopping or deciding what to eat. The impact of this law will be felt across the country, affecting people of all age groups, and some may be left wondering what to say on the tip of their tongue. \nP.P.S As the country watches Oregon's new law unfold, it will be interesting to see how it affects the lives of its citizens, from young to old, and whether it will lead to a shift in the way people think about addiction and treatment.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.3132617473602295}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.001680035027